# a-wall-against-a-miner — plan 23 K0.
#
# One miner, one sealed corridor, and a wall that comes down in
# EIGHT ticks of siege where a robot needs twenty-three.
#
# ── Read it beside its twin ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# `a-wall-against-a-scout.keys` is this file with one word changed —
# `wave 1 miner` becomes `wave 1 scout` — and the same wall in the
# same corridor at the same moment reads 12.9-13.1 HP where this one
# reads 8.9-9.1 after three ticks and is RUBBLE after eight.  The
# pair is the measurement; neither file is one on its own.
#
# That is `docs/DESIGN.md` § Small robots stated as two runs: *"the
# same wave arriving at the same perimeter is a very different
# problem depending on what is in it"*.  A miner cuts rock for a
# living and a scout has no real weapon at all.
#
# ── Why ONE enemy, and why a one-hex corridor ───────────────────
#
# Both are deliberate, and both are about keeping the reading
# attributable to the CLASS:
#
#   - **one enemy**, because the breach clock SATURATES past a
#     handful of attackers (`numbers_design_targets.loft` § the
#     breach clock saturates) — with four, two miners already break
#     the hex as fast as four do, and the rate stops being visible;
#   - **a one-hex corridor**, because a wall FACE has ends worth 30%
#     and a middle worth 100% (plan 12 B3) and the siege chews where
#     the ROUTE meets it — so a wide band measures the geometry as
#     much as the class.  A stub in a corridor is 15 HP with nothing
#     to lean on and exactly one hex anybody can attack.
#
# ── The world (identical to its twin) ───────────────────────────
#
#   one corridor of grass, twelve hexes:  (12, 0) -> (0, 0)
#   sealed at (2, 0) with a wall          15 HP — a STUB, unbraced
#   core at (0, 0), spawn at (12, 0)

echo a wall against a miner: 3 HP/s, and the seal does not last

do toggle_mode

# The core is where the target is.
do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# The spawn, twelve hexes out — clear of close_spawn_disable_radius
# (10) and inside the scrambler bubble (25).
# ⚠ TWICE: the place-kind cycle is spawn -> target -> tower -> spawn
# since plan 12 B5a gave towers a marker kind of their own.
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 12 0
marker 12 0 spawn 3

count markers 2 2
do toggle_mode

palette 5
drag 12 0 0 0
count painted 13 13

palette 9
click 2 0
kind 2 0 wall
count painted 13 13

# ⚠ **15 HP, not 100** — the seal is a STUB with no structure
# neighbours to push against (plan 12 B3).  Same figure as its twin,
# which is what makes the two HP readings comparable at all.
wave 1 miner
count alive 1 1
count targets 0 0
hp 2 0 15.0 15.0

# The pre-walk window (plan 16 W2): 8 ticks standing at the marker,
# and a standing enemy chews nothing whatever its class.
tick 8
enemy 0 12 0
hp 2 0 15.0 15.0

# FOURTEEN ticks to arrive at (3, 0) — nine hexes at 1.0 hex/s, where
# a robot needs nine and a scout six (plan 23 K2b).  ⚠ The tick that
# CARRIES it there is one in which it could still move, and an enemy
# that can move attacks nothing — so the wall is untouched at the
# moment the siege begins.
#
# ⚠ **This line was `tick 9` until K2b and the HP lines below did not
# move with it**, which is the property worth reading off the pair: a
# class's speed decides when the siege STARTS and its bite decides how
# the siege goes, and the two axes do not contaminate each other.
tick 14
enemy 0 3 0
enemies passable
count targets 1 1
target 0 2 0
hp 2 0 15.0 15.0

# ── Three ticks of siege, and this is the whole phase ───────────
#
# 3 x per_tick(3.0) = 6.0 HP off a 15 HP stub.  Its twin, at the same
# moment, has lost 0.2.
tick 3
hp 2 0 8.9 9.1
enemy 0 3 0

# ── And through ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Five more ticks is 10 HP against the 9 that are left, so the seal
# goes down into a heap of masonry and the miner walks over it —
# EIGHT ticks of siege, unaided.  `a-wall-breaks.keys` had to soften
# its wall with a `damage` line to reach here at all, because a robot
# would have needed twenty-three.
tick 5
kind 2 0 grass
pile 2 0 0.9 1.1
count targets 0 0

snap k0-the-miner-on-the-breach

# Onto the heap first — a 0.99 m step, inside a miner's 2.0 m climb,
# which is the regular's climb because all four small robots share it
# (`passable.loft` § The four small robots have no row here).
#
# ⚠ **One tick is one hex only because the bank happens to owe one
# here.**  A miner at 1.0 hex/s owes a whole hex on two ticks in every
# three (plan 23 K2b), so a reading like this is a statement about
# WHICH tick as much as about how many — which is why the last two
# hexes below take three ticks and not two.
tick 1
enemy 0 2 0
enemies passable

tick 3
enemy 0 0 0
count alive 1 1
range 0 0
